t from 1587 to 1612.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vincenzo-scamozzi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vincenzo-scamozzi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vincenzo Scamozzi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vincenzo Scamozzi was an Italian architect from Vicenza, the leading successor of Andrea Palladio.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vindhya-range"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vindhya-range.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vindhya Range</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vindhya Range is a broken chain of hills and plateau scarps across west-central India, running roughly east to west north of the Narmada valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vindhyashakti"><span class="enc-name">Vindhyashakti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vindhyashakti was the founder of the Vakataka dynasty, which rose in the Deccan of India in the mid-3rd century as the Satavahana dynasty broke up.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vindolanda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vindolanda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vindolanda</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vindolanda was a Roman auxiliary fort in northern England, just south of Hadrian's Wall in Northumberland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vindonissa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vindonissa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vindonissa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vindonissa was a Roman legionary fortress at present-day Windisch, in the canton of Aargau, Switzerland, near the confluence of the Aare and the Reuss.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vinh-te-canal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vinh-te-canal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vĩnh Tế Canal</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vĩnh Tế Canal is a canal of about 90 kilometres in the Mekong delta, running along the Cambodian border from Châu Đốc to Hà Tiên on the Gulf of Thailand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vinicio-cerezo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vinicio-cerezo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vinicio Cerezo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vinicio Cerezo (born 1942) is a Guatemalan Christian Democrat politician who was president from 1986 to 1991, the first civilian elected under the constitution of 1985…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vinschgau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vinschgau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vinschgau</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vinschgau (Italian Val Venosta) is the valley of the upper Adige in South Tyrol, northern Italy, running from the Reschen Pass down to Merano.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/violeta-chamorro"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/violeta-chamorro.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Violeta Chamorro</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Violeta Barrios de Chamorro was a Nicaraguan publisher and politician who was president of Nicaragua from 1990 to 1997, the first woman elected president in the Americas.</span></a></li></ul>
